###############################################################################
# Set default behavior to automatically normalize line endings.
#
# This is a Windows-only project and several consumers REQUIRE CRLF in the
# working tree regardless of core.autocrlf:
#   - RegressionTests Resources\*.eml contain DKIM-signed messages whose body
#     hashes break if line endings change
#   - Raw-message tests send file/string content verbatim over SMTP; bare LFs
#     are rejected with "554 Message containing bare LF's"
# The database scripts are no longer among them: SQLScriptParser folds CRLF to
# LF and then splits a script into statements at a blank line (";" and a blank
# line for PostgreSQL), so a script parses with either ending.
# Hence: normalize text in the repo, but ALWAYS check out CRLF.
###############################################################################
* text=auto eol=crlf

###############################################################################
# The exception: everything a POSIX machine EXECUTES.
#
# The rule above is right for a Windows-only project and wrong for the moment
# one of its files is run by /bin/sh. A maintainer script checked out with CRLF
# fails as "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M" when the package is installed, and the
# failure is at the user's machine rather than here. The systemd unit, the
# logrotate rule and the packaged configuration are read by parsers that are
# equally unforgiving, and the AppImage and PKGBUILD are shell.
#
# These are checked out LF on every platform, including Windows, where nothing
# reads them but an editor.
###############################################################################
hmailserver/source/Server/platform/packaging/** text eol=lf
build/*.sh text eol=lf
# The Linux CI jobs' scripts and their image's Dockerfile, which bash and
# docker build read on the project's own runner (.gitlab-ci.yml).
build/ci/*.sh text eol=lf
build/ci/*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
# The test lab on linix (build/lab/README.md): its scripts, which bash, node and
# python3 run there, and the compose file and the configuration files the
# containers read.
build/lab/** text eol=lf
# And the command-line client, which the .deb and the .rpm install as
# /usr/bin/hmctl: it opens with a shebang, so a CRLF checkout ships a file that
# fails as "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env python3^M" on the user's machine and
# nowhere else.
hmailserver/source/Tools/Cli/hmctl text eol=lf

###############################################################################
# The OpenPGP fixtures, byte for byte.
#
# These are keys, signatures and messages GnuPG 2.4 made, and two whole
# PGP/MIME messages whose signed part is signed over its exact octets. A
# signature does not survive a line ending being rewritten under it, and the
# rule at the top of this file would rewrite every one of them on checkout, so
# they are marked binary: no normalisation, no diff, the bytes GnuPG wrote.
###############################################################################
build/fixtures/openpgp/** binary

###############################################################################
# The PST fixtures: Outlook data files build/generate-pst-fixtures.py writes, whose
# every byte is covered by a CRC. Binary, as any .pst anywhere is.
###############################################################################
build/fixtures/pst/** binary
*.pst binary

# Explicitly binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.pfx binary
*.snk binary
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.gif binary
*.bmp binary
*.msi binary
*.tlb binary
*.7z  binary
*.zip binary

